const faqItems = [
  { question: "Does IRC 280E still apply to Maine cannabis businesses in 2026?", answer: "It depends. State-licensed medical cannabis — including products sold by registered Maine caregivers and licensed dispensaries — was moved to Schedule III of the federal Controlled Substances Act on April 28, 2026, by the U.S. Department of Justice. For that medical side, 280E is no longer the binding federal rule; standard Section 162 business deductions apply. Adult-use (recreational) cannabis remains on Schedule I until the DEA's broader rescheduling hearing concludes, currently scheduled to end no later than July 15, 2026. Dual-license operators must therefore keep 280E compliance for their adult-use books while reclaiming ordinary deductions on the medical side." },
  { question: "What changed for Maine dual-license operators on April 28, 2026?", answer: "That is the effective date of the DOJ Final Order (AG Order No. 6754-2026, published at 91 Federal Register 22714). The order moved two categories of marijuana to Schedule III: (1) marijuana contained in an FDA-approved drug product, and (2) marijuana subject to a state-issued medical marijuana license. Maine caregiver storefronts, registered caregivers, and licensed medical dispensaries all qualify under the second category. The order also opened an expedited DEA registration pathway (21 CFR Part 1301) for medical licensees; applications filed within 60 days of publication — by June 27, 2026 — receive priority six-month processing and may operate under their state-issued licenses during the application pendency." },
  { question: "What is the dual-license 280E expense apportionment strategy?", answer: "It is the practice of allocating shared overhead — rent, payroll, security, insurance, utilities, marketing, software, professional services — between Schedule III medical operations and Schedule I adult-use operations on a reasonable, contemporaneously-documented basis, so that the portion of the expense attributable to medical activity is deducted under Section 162 rather than trapped in 280E. Federal appellate precedent (Californians Helping to Alleviate Med. Problems v. Commissioner, 128 T.C. 173) and IRS audit guidance both support cost-allocation methods that use revenue, square footage, employee time, or another measurable driver, so long as the methodology is documented and consistently applied." },
  { question: "When does the expedited DEA registration window close?", answer: "Medical cannabis entities that submit their DEA registration application within 60 days of the April 28, 2026 Federal Register publication — by close of business June 27, 2026 — qualify for the expedited six-month review. Maine dual-license operators with a Schedule III medical component should treat this as a hard deadline. Late filings still go through the standard 21 CFR Part 1301 process but without the priority lane and without the explicit statutory permission to operate under the state license during review." },
  { question: "Does the June 29, 2026 DEA hearing change anything for adult-use?", answer: "Not immediately. The hearing (AG Order No. 6753-2026, 91 FR 22778) is the formal administrative proceeding on the proposed rescheduling of all marijuana from Schedule I to Schedule III. It begins June 29, 2026 at the DEA Hearing Facility in Arlington, Virginia, and must conclude no later than July 15, 2026. The hearing is procedural — the final rescheduling rule for adult-use comes after the ALJ issues a recommended decision, then a final DEA order, then publication in the Federal Register. Operators should not assume a specific date for adult-use Schedule III; build a tax strategy that works whether the broader rescheduling lands in late 2026, 2027, or later." },
  { question: "Can a Maine operator keep one legal entity for both license types?", answer: "Yes. Schedule III is determined at the product level, not the entity level, so a single LLC can hold both a caregiver or medical dispensary license and an adult-use retail license. The apportionment work is the same: keep separate books, separate Metrc tag streams where feasible, separate inventory accounts, and a written allocation policy. That said, many sophisticated operators use a holding-company structure with a medical LLC and an adult-use LLC for risk and banking isolation. Either approach works for tax purposes; the entity choice should be driven by your banker, your insurer, and your litigation posture, not by the tax code." }
];

